{
"A sentence that states its importance flatly.": "12ff69d49b39",
"Aristotle gave two of the ten books of his Ethics to friendship, more than to any other single subject, and he opens with a sentence that states its importance flatly: without friends no one would choose to live, though he had all other goods. The rich need friends in order to have someone to benefit, the poor need them as a refuge, the young to keep them from error, and the old to care for them. Modern moral philosophy has had much less to say. Its main theories are about what we owe to everyone, and a friend is someone to whom we give what we do not owe and from whom we expect what we could not demand. The ancient writers, Greek and Chinese, treated the subject as central, and the best way into it is still Aristotles question: what is it that we like when we like a person?": "f873650e41af",
"Aristotles account makes friendship reasonable: we love the good because it is good. The French essayist Michel de Montaigne, who had one such friend and lost him, thought that this leaves out the center of the experience. He met Étienne de La Boétie at a crowded feast in Bordeaux when both were young magistrates, and from that first meeting, he says, nothing was so near to either as the other. La Boétie died a few years later, at thirty-two. Montaigne wrote the essay on friendship some ten years afterward. In such a friendship, he says, two souls mix and work themselves into one piece, with so universal a mixture, that there is no more sign of the seam by which they were first conjoined. Asked for the reason, he can give none. If a man should importune me to give a reason why I loved him, I find it could no otherwise be expressed, than by making answer: because it was he, because it was I.": "2b56e7ec5d2f",
"Confucius approaches the matter as a teacher would, by asking what a friend does to you. Three kinds of friends are a gain and three are a loss. To have friends who are upright, friends who are true to their word, and friends who have heard much is a gain. To have friends who are smooth, friends who are soft and pleasing, and friends who are glib is a loss. The helpful friend tells you the truth, can be relied on, and knows things you do not. The harmful ones are, in Aristotles terms, friends of pleasure. They agree with you. His student Zengzi states the purpose in a line: the gentleman uses his friends to support his humaneness. A friend is a partner in becoming a better person, and friendship is the one relationship among the five Confucian bonds that is chosen and that holds between equals.": "f3fb1907a29e",
"Dunbars number": "0c33d1d63904",
"For lay life the Buddha gave advice that reads like a longer version of the Analects passage. He tells the young householder Sigālaka to recognize four enemies disguised as friends, among them the one who only takes and the flatterer, and four good-hearted friends: the helper, the friend in good times and bad, the counselor, and the one whos sympathetic. The counselor is described in words Confucius could have used: They keep you from doing bad. They support you in doing good. They teach you what you do not know. The tradition adds one thing the philosophers lack. Loving-kindness, mettā, comes from the same root as mitta, friend. It is friendliness trained until it no longer depends on who the other person is, and it is to be extended in the end to strangers and enemies. Aristotle says that we can be friends in the full sense with very few. The Buddha agrees about friendship and holds that the goodwill at its center can be made boundless.": "978598a68257",
"Friends who are glib are a loss.": "1ce674d4ad74",
"Has this friend stood by you when it cost something?": "72c00f11828e",
"If a man should importune me to give a reason.": "877f70b6d060",
"In virtue of some good which they get from each other.": "49b9797257c9",
"No more sign of the seam by which they were joined.": "922fe37f8135",
"Perfect friendship is the friendship of men who are good, and alike in virtue; for these wish well alike to each other qua good, and they are good themselves. Now those who wish well to their friends for their sake are most truly friends; for they do this by reason of own nature and not incidentally; therefore their friendship lasts as long as they are good—and goodness is an enduring thing.": "aeb457c21bf3",
"That leaves a question the exercise below is meant to raise. Most of the people we call friends are friends of use or of pleasure, and there is nothing wrong with that. The difficulty comes from not knowing which is which. We are hurt when a friendship of convenience ends with the convenience, and we neglect, because it is plain as water, the one that would have lasted.": "ed51656fa33a",
"The Buddhas attendant Ānanda once remarked that good friendship is half of the spiritual life. Not so, Ānanda! Not so, Ānanda! Good friends, companions, and associates are the whole of the spiritual life. A person with good friends can be expected to develop the path, and the Buddha describes himself as the good friend by relying on whom beings are freed. The Pali term is kalyāṇamitta, the beautiful or admirable friend. It is close to Aristotles friendship of character, with the goal moved: the friend is the one who helps you toward liberation, where Aristotles helps you toward a flourishing life in the city.": "ccf2a53c8e6c",
"The Chinese tradition has its own word for Montaignes kind of friend, and it comes from a story. Bo Ya was a master of the zither, and Zhong Ziqi was good at listening. When Bo Ya played with his mind on high mountains, Zhong Ziqi said, Splendid! Lofty as Mount Tai! When his mind was on flowing water, Zhong Ziqi said, Splendid! Broad as the great rivers! Whatever Bo Ya had in mind, Zhong Ziqi never failed to grasp it. When Zhong Ziqi died, another version adds, Bo Ya broke the zither and cut its strings and never played again, judging that there was no one left in the world worth playing for. The phrase zhīyīn, one who knows the tone, has meant a true friend ever since. What the story picks out is being understood. Zhong Ziqi did nothing for Bo Ya and gave him no pleasure that a paying audience could not have given. He heard what was meant.": "63a946fb38c1",
"The disagreement is real. For Aristotle a friend is loved for qualities, and anyone with the same qualities would in principle do as well. For Montaigne the friend cannot be replaced, and a list of his virtues would explain nothing. Both seem to be describing something true. We do admire our closest friends, and we would be dismayed to learn that they were cruel. Yet we would not exchange them for someone more admirable.": "f0eb9783db1d",
"The historian Sima Qian preserves a second example in the words of the statesman Guan Zhong. When he was poor and took the larger share of their profits, his friend Bao Shuya did not think him greedy, knowing that he was poor. When he fled three times in battle, Bao did not think him a coward, knowing that he had an old mother. It was my parents who gave me life, Guan Zhong said, and it is Bao who knows me. To be known in this way is to be seen at ones worst and read correctly. It asks more of a friend than Aristotles likeness in virtue.": "38af84a5e65d",
"The numbers have held up fairly well.": "0fbc71808f9c",
"The poor need friends as a refuge.": "0e15eb8675d6",
"The third kind is different. Perfect friendship is the friendship of men who are good, and alike in virtue, and such friends wish well to their friends for their sake. Here the person himself is what is loved, his character and not his services. This friendship includes the other two, since good people are both useful and pleasant to each other, and it lasts, because character is the most durable thing about a person. It is also rare. It needs time and shared life, for men cannot know each other till they have eaten salt together, and nobody can have it with many people, any more than one can be in love with many at once. Aristotle adds a phrase that has been repeated ever since: a friend of this kind is another self.": "46d2deaee633",
"The word has recently been stretched. A person may now have a thousand friends on a screen, most of whom he has not eaten salt with. The anthropologist Robin Dunbar has argued, from the size of primate brains and from counts of human groups, that a person can keep up stable relationships with about a hundred and fifty people, of whom about fifteen are close and about five are intimate. The numbers have held up fairly well in studies of telephone records and of online networks. They are Aristotles point in figures: the highest kind of friendship needs shared time, and time is limited.": "4d275e8547c2",
"There are three things, he says, for which we can love anything: because it is useful, because it is pleasant, or because it is good. There are therefore three kinds of friendship. In the first, each party values the other for some advantage. Business partners are friends in this way, and so are many neighbors and colleagues. In the second, each enjoys the others company: the people we drink, travel, or play with. Aristotle does not despise these. He observes two things about them. In both, the other person is loved for something he supplies, and so they last only as long as the supply. Those who love each other for their utility do not love each other for themselves but in virtue of some good which they get from each other. When the usefulness ends or tastes change, the friendship dissolves, and no one is to blame.": "1e27aac4f29c",
"We would be dismayed to learn that they were cruel.": "52f047452234",
"When the usefulness ends, the friendship dissolves.": "dc3a07a9edde",
"Zhuangzi offers a caution about warmth. The friendship of the gentleman is plain as water. The friendship of the small man is sweet as rich wine. The gentlemans plainness brings closeness. The small mans sweetness ends in a break. A relation that needs constant sweetness is being held together by something other than the two people.": "f336baac9f83",
